{{package}}

import dev.ravel.jvm.Hook;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
{{import Minecraft}}
{{import Button}}
// The binary name is the type's name here, for the reason RavelSettingsScreen
// records: the pinned version reaches every interactive widget through a nested
// type and the stubs carry the binary name the JVM links on.
{{import Button$OnPress}}
{{import StringWidget}}
{{import Screen}}
{{import Component}}

/**
 * Ravel's mod list and dependency diagnostics screen.
 *
 * <p>{@code ROADMAP.md}'s M3-W5 row asks for a mod list and diagnostics UI over
 * the model {@code ravel-loader} already holds, ordered by resolved load order,
 * and for valid and invalid graphs to render deterministic, actionable messages.
 * ADG-6 settled the drawing half before this screen existed: it names M3-W5's
 * mod list as <i>the same mechanism</i> as the settings screen rather than a
 * second one. So this is that mechanism again — one batched read on the same
 * length-prefixed framing, the same widget vocabulary, and no drawing outside
 * this class.
 *
 * <p><b>One crossing, not two, and scrolling does not add a third.</b> A mod
 * list is read and never written, so the session makes a single call:
 * {@code Hook.ravelModSurface} in {@link #init}. Scrolling re-enters {@code init}
 * through {@link #rebuildWidgets}, so the read is guarded by {@link #loaded} and
 * happens once per instance however far a player scrolls. Nothing crosses per
 * row and nothing per frame. {@link #loaded} is set from the read rather than
 * before it, so a payload that was refused is read again on the next layout: a
 * refusal latched for the life of the instance would keep the screen saying the
 * list could not be read after the reason it could not had gone.
 *
 * <p><b>The sentence is composed here.</b> A failure arrives as attribution —
 * kind, surface, dependency chain, the state it failed in, the detail — rather
 * than as text already flattened into a sentence, because flattening it on the
 * Rust side would have taken the choice of what to show away from the only part
 * of Ravel that knows what a player is looking at. The composing is
 * {@link Entry#lines}, and it is the only place a message is built.
 *
 * <p><b>Actionable and reportable are drawn differently on purpose.</b> A failed
 * required mod is actionable: the set cannot open a world without it, so it is
 * marked and counted in the summary. A failed optional mod is reported and not
 * marked, because the plan already tolerates its absence and telling a player to
 * fix it would be asking for work the loader does not need done. That is the
 * difference between a diagnostic and a nag, and it is the loader's distinction
 * rather than this screen's — {@code ModEntry.is_actionable} decides it and this
 * screen only draws what it decided.
 *
 * <p><b>A notice is drawn above the rows and counted with them.</b> The launch
 * records things a player is told without being refused — a resource pack that
 * was written and could not be switched on is the one there is — and those are
 * about the launch rather than about any one mod, so they sit above the rows
 * with no row of their own. The summary counts them, because that line is what a
 * player reads before deciding nothing is wrong. Each is split across as many
 * rows as its text needs: a notice says what happened <i>and</i> what can be
 * done about it, and a row given more text than it has width shows only part of
 * it, so a notice on one row is a notice whose remedy is out of sight.
 *
 * <p><b>Everything below the summary is one scrolling list.</b> The notice
 * lines, the mod rows and the settings entries are flattened into {@link #rows}
 * in that order, and the window draws the slice of it starting at
 * {@link #scroll}. That replaces a row budget divided between the three groups,
 * which was the design launch 20 met: at the game's own GUI scale this screen
 * has about six rows, and dividing them showed one line of a seven-line notice
 * and cut the settings entry. Nothing is cut now — a row a window has no space
 * for is a row a player scrolls to — and the summary says which slice is on
 * screen so that a player knows there is more.
 *
 * <p><b>Rows are drawn actionable-first, and every row states its load
 * position.</b> The two go together and the second is what makes the first safe.
 * The model stays in resolved load order — that is what {@code surface.rs}
 * promises and nothing here changes it — but the rows a player has to act on are
 * the rows the screen exists to show, so they are the ones it opens on rather
 * than the ones a player has to scroll for. Grouping is still deterministic: the
 * same set always draws the same screen. The position is numbered on every row,
 * not only on moved ones, so a player reads the load order from the numbers
 * rather than inferring it from where a row happens to sit. Without the numbers
 * this reordering would not be defensible.
 *
 * <p><b>The wheel is not the only way down the list.</b> Up and Down move it by
 * a row and the page keys by a window, which is what a player without a wheel
 * has. The keystroke arrives through {@link RavelScrolling}, whose only reason
 * to exist is that the two pinned versions declare {@code keyPressed} with
 * different parameters. A key this screen does not use goes to the game, so
 * escape still closes the screen and tab still moves the focus.
 *
 * <p><b>A layout puts the focus back where it was.</b> The game's
 * {@code rebuildWidgets} clears the focus and sets the initial one, so a player
 * who tabbed to a settings entry and then pressed Down would have been moved off
 * it by the very layout that scrolled. This screen reads the focused widget
 * before the layout and focuses it again afterwards: a settings entry whenever
 * the window it lands on still draws one, Profiling and Done always.
 *
 * <p>Every widget here is one the game builds without this class drawing
 * anything, for the reason {@link RavelSettingsScreen} records at length:
 * {@code AbstractButton} declares {@code extractContents} and implementing it
 * would be drawing, which is the one thing ADG-6 forbids. A text row is a
 * {@code StringWidget}, which is the game's own read-only label: it takes no
 * input, so it draws no caret and no focus border and swallows no click, which
 * an uneditable text field did all three of.
 */
public final class RavelModsScreen extends RavelScrolling {

    /** What marks a line as one a player has to act on. */
    private static final String ATTENTION = "[needs attention] ";

    // The key codes the game reports, which are the window system's own and not
    // the game's, so they are the same number under every version Ravel pins.
    private static final int KEY_UP = 265;
    private static final int KEY_DOWN = 264;
    private static final int KEY_PAGE_UP = 266;
    private static final int KEY_PAGE_DOWN = 267;

    /** The mod set as it stood when this screen opened. */
    private final List<Entry> entries = new ArrayList<>();

    /** What the launch told the player without refusing them, as it stood then. */
    private final List<String> notices = new ArrayList<>();

    /** Every row those notices were split into, whether the window draws it or not. */
    private final List<String> noticeRows = new ArrayList<>();

    /** Everything below the summary, in the one order it is scrolled through. */
    private final List<Row> rows = new ArrayList<>();

    /** The settings entry this screen last drew for each mod that has one. */
    private final Map<String, {{Button}}> drawnSettings = new LinkedHashMap<>();

    /** Which row of {@link #rows} the window starts at. */
    private int scroll;

    /** The Profiling and Done buttons this screen last drew, in that order. */
    private final List<{{Button}}> below = new ArrayList<>();

    /** How many entries the loader says a player has to act on. */
    private int actionable;

    /** The side the set is running on, as the loader reported it. */
    private String side = "";

    /** Whether the surface has been read, which happens once per instance. */
    private boolean loaded;

    /** Whether the payload read at all. */
    private boolean read;

    private RavelModsScreen() {
        super({{Component}}.{{Component#translatable}}("ravel.mods.title"));
    }

    /**
     * Builds the screen.
     *
     * <p>Called reflectively by {@code dev.ravel.jvm.Screens}, which cannot name
     * this type: {@code Screens} lives in the agent jar and ADG-1 forbids that
     * jar naming a game type, which this class's supertype is.
     *
     * @return a screen the caller hands to the game
     */
    public static {{Screen}} open() {
        return new RavelModsScreen();
    }

    /** The mod set this screen read, in resolved load order. */
    public List<Entry> entries() {
        return List.copyOf(entries);
    }

    /** How many entries a player has to act on. */
    public int actionable() {
        return actionable;
    }

    /** What the launch told the player without refusing them. */
    public List<String> notices() {
        return List.copyOf(notices);
    }

    /**
     * Every row those notices were split into, drawn or scrolled past.
     *
     * <p>The whole split rather than the part on screen, which is what a caller
     * needs to tell a drawn notice line apart from a drawn mod line: the window
     * is a slice of one list and a line's group is not readable from where it
     * sits. What is on screen is {@link #rowLabels} and the offset.
     */
    public List<String> noticeRows() {
        return List.copyOf(noticeRows);
    }

    /** Every row below the summary, drawn or scrolled past, as its label reads. */
    public List<String> rowLabels() {
        List<String> labels = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Row row : rows) {
            labels.add(row.label());
        }
        return labels;
    }

    /**
     * Reads the mod set once and lays the window out over the rows it built.
     *
     * <p>A payload that does not read leaves the list empty and says so rather
     * than drawing part of one. Half a mod list is worse than none: it looks
     * like the set is smaller than it is, which is the opposite of a diagnostic.
     *
     * <p>{@link #rebuildWidgets} re-enters this method for every scrolled row,
     * so the read is behind {@link #loaded} and the rows are rebuilt from what it
     * already holds. Reading again would put a second crossing on a keystroke.
     */
    @Override
    protected void {{Screen#init}}() {
        super.{{Screen#init}}();
        if (!loaded) {
            read = parse(Hook.ravelModSurface());
            loaded = read;
        }

        rows.clear();
        noticeRows.clear();
        drawnSettings.clear();
        below.clear();
        for (String notice : notices) {
            for (String line : Layout.wrap({{Screen#font}}, ATTENTION + notice, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH)) {
                noticeRows.add(line);
                rows.add(Row.line(line));
            }
        }
        for (Entry entry : ordered()) {
            for (String line : entry.lines()) {
                for (String wrapped : Layout.wrap({{Screen#font}}, line, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH)) {
                    rows.add(Row.line(wrapped));
                }
            }
        }
        for (Entry entry : configurable()) {
            rows.add(Row.settings(entry.modId));
        }

        int shown = shown();
        // A window that grew can show rows the old offset put past the end, so
        // the offset is bounded on every layout and not only on a scroll.
        scroll = bound(scroll, shown);

        int y = Layout.TOP;
        {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(label(summary(), y));
        y += Layout.ROW;
        for (int index = scroll; index < scroll + shown; index++) {
            Row row = rows.get(index);
            if (row.owner == null) {
                {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(label(row.text, y));
            } else {
                {{Button}} entry = settings(row.owner, y);
                drawnSettings.put(row.owner, entry);
                {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(entry);
            }
            y += Layout.ROW;
        }

        below.add(
                {{Button}}.{{Button#builder}}({{Component}}.{{Component#literal}}("Profiling"), new {{Button$OnPress}}() {
                            @Override
                            public void {{Button$OnPress#onPress}}({{Button}} pressed) {
                                {{Minecraft}}.{{Minecraft#getInstance}}().{{Minecraft#setScreen}}(RavelMenu.openProfiling(RavelModsScreen.this));
                            }
                        })
                        .{{Button$Builder#bounds}}(centre(), y, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT)
                        .{{Button$Builder#build}}());
        y += Layout.ROW;
        below.add(
                {{Button}}.{{Button#builder}}({{Component}}.{{Component#translatable}}("gui.done"), new {{Button$OnPress}}() {
                            @Override
                            public void {{Button$OnPress#onPress}}({{Button}} pressed) {
                                {{Screen#onClose}}();
                            }
                        })
                        .{{Button$Builder#bounds}}(centre(), y + Layout.ROW, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT)
                        .{{Button$Builder#build}}());
        for ({{Button}} button : below) {
            {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(button);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Moves the window by one row a notch and draws it again.
     *
     * <p>A notch with no vertical component is not this screen's: a sideways
     * wheel would otherwise lay the whole screen out to move nothing and then
     * claim the event, which is a layout a player asked for nothing by.
     *
     * @return whether this screen used the event
     */
    @Override
    public boolean {{GuiEventListener#mouseScrolled}}(double mouseX, double mouseY, double scrollX, double scrollY) {
        return move(-(int) Math.signum(scrollY));
    }

    /**
     * Moves the window for one key, or leaves it where it is.
     *
     * <p>A page is the window, so the key that follows a page ends where the eye
     * left off. Every other key is the game's.
     *
     * @param key the key code the game reported
     * @return whether the key moved the window
     */
    @Override
    boolean scrolled(int key) {
        if (key == KEY_UP) {
            return move(-1);
        }
        if (key == KEY_DOWN) {
            return move(1);
        }
        if (key == KEY_PAGE_UP) {
            return move(-Math.max(1, shown()));
        }
        if (key == KEY_PAGE_DOWN) {
            return move(Math.max(1, shown()));
        }
        return false;
    }

    /**
     * Moves the offset by rows and draws the screen again.
     *
     * <p>Refused when the whole list is already on screen, so that a window with
     * nothing to scroll leaves the event to whatever the game does with it rather
     * than swallowing it to do nothing. Refused too when the window has room for
     * no row at all: there is nothing to look at, so moving the offset would only
     * leave a taller window opening part of the way down a list a player never
     * scrolled, and so is a notch past either end: the window is already where
     * that notch would put it, and laying it out again would be a layout a
     * player asked for nothing by. {@link #init} bounds the offset as well,
     * because a window that changed size has to be bounded on a layout no wheel
     * caused.
     *
     * @param by how many rows to move, positive being further down the list
     * @return whether the window moved
     */
    private boolean move(int by) {
        int shown = shown();
        if (by == 0 || shown <= 0 || rows.size() <= shown) {
            return false;
        }
        int to = bound(scroll + by, shown);
        if (to == scroll) {
            return false;
        }
        scroll = to;
        relayout();
        return true;
    }

    /**
     * The offset the list has room for nearest {@code offset}.
     *
     * <p>The bound is the last row rather than the last window, because a window
     * with no room for a row at all has no last window and would otherwise park
     * the offset past the whole list.
     */
    private int bound(int offset, int shown) {
        return Math.max(0, Math.min(offset, rows.size() - Math.max(shown, 1)));
    }

    /**
     * Lays the screen out again and puts the focus back where it was.
     *
     * <p>The game's own layout clears the focus and sets the initial one, which
     * on a screen scrolled by the keyboard would move a player off the entry they
     * were on every time they pressed a key. An entry is found again by the mod
     * it opens rather than by where it sat, because the window it lands in is a
     * different slice of the list; Profiling and Done are found by which of the
     * two they are, because every layout draws both.
     */
    private void relayout() {
        String owner = focusedOwner();
        int held = below.indexOf({{AbstractContainerEventHandler#getFocused}}());
        {{Screen#rebuildWidgets}}();
        {{Button}} again = owner != null ? drawnSettings.get(owner) : held < 0 ? null : below.get(held);
        if (again != null) {
            {{AbstractContainerEventHandler#setFocused}}(again);
        }
    }

    /** The mod whose settings entry holds the focus, or {@code null} if none does. */
    private String focusedOwner() {
        Object focused = {{AbstractContainerEventHandler#getFocused}}();
        for (Map.Entry<String, {{Button}}> drawn : drawnSettings.entrySet()) {
            if (drawn.getValue() == focused) {
                return drawn.getKey();
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    /**
     * How many rows of the list this window draws.
     *
     * <p>The rows the window has, less the one the summary takes, and never more
     * than the list holds. Two more rows are reserved below the list for
     * Profiling and Done, which is what keeps them on screen whatever is drawn
     * above them. {@link Layout#rows} measures the rest.
     *
     * @return how many rows of {@link #rows} are on screen, which can be none
     */
    private int shown() {
        return Math.min(rows.size(), Math.max(0, Layout.rows({{Screen#height}}, 2) - 1));
    }

    /**
     * The set with actionable entries first, each group keeping load order.
     *
     * <p>Load order is what the row asks the list to be in, and it still is
     * within each group. Putting the entries a player has to act on at the top is
     * what makes them the rows the screen opens on rather than rows a player has
     * to find.
     */
    private List<Entry> ordered() {
        List<Entry> sorted = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Entry entry : entries) {
            if (entry.actionable) {
                sorted.add(entry);
            }
        }
        for (Entry entry : entries) {
            if (!entry.actionable) {
                sorted.add(entry);
            }
        }
        return sorted;
    }

    /**
     * The mods a settings screen has something to draw for, in load order.
     *
     * <p>Load order rather than the actionable-first order above, because a
     * settings entry is not a diagnostic and a player looks for it by name.
     */
    private List<Entry> configurable() {
        List<Entry> offered = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Entry entry : entries) {
            if (entry.configurable) {
                offered.add(entry);
            }
        }
        return offered;
    }

    /**
     * The line above the list, saying what the player is looking at.
     *
     * <p>The count includes a notice as well as a failed mod. It is followed by
     * which part of the list is on screen, whenever that is not all of it: a
     * player who reads that three things need attention and sees two rows has
     * been told the screen is complete, which is the one thing this line must
     * never say when it is not. Where a row budget used to report what it had cut
     * for good, this reports where a player is in a list nothing was cut from.
     *
     * <p>The clause is {@code rows 1-8 of 14} rather than a sentence because this
     * line is one row and can outgrow it. The window this screen opens at draws
     * about fifty characters, a longer wording pushed the clause off the end, and
     * a position a player cannot read is worse than none.
     *
     * @return the line
     */
    private String summary() {
        if (!read) {
            return "The mod list could not be read.";
        }
        int attention = actionable + notices.size();
        StringBuilder line =
                new StringBuilder(
                        entries.isEmpty()
                                ? "No mods are installed"
                                : plural(entries.size(), "mod", "mods") + " on " + side);
        if (attention == 0) {
            line.append(entries.isEmpty() ? "." : ", none needing attention");
        } else {
            line.append(", ").append(plural(attention, "needs", "need")).append(" attention");
        }
        int shown = shown();
        if (shown == 0 && !rows.isEmpty()) {
            line.append(", no room for ").append(plural(rows.size(), "row", "rows"));
        } else if (shown < rows.size()) {
            line.append(", rows ")
                    .append(scroll + 1)
                    .append('-')
                    .append(scroll + shown)
                    .append(" of ")
                    .append(rows.size());
        }
        return line.toString();
    }

    /**
     * One line of text, which is how this screen shows a row.
     *
     * <p>The game's read-only label rather than an uneditable text field. The
     * field was the choice while every row was one, and it costs a caret, a focus
     * border and a swallowed click on a row a player cannot type in — all three
     * of which launch 20 saw on screen. This widget takes no input at all, and it
     * draws its text against its whole width rather than inside a border, which
     * is why {@link Layout#wrap} is asked for the full width here.
     *
     * <p>The box is the width of the text it holds rather than the width of the
     * column, and that is what left-aligns the row. One pinned version centres a
     * label inside its box and the other draws it at the box's left edge, and
     * they share no member that says which — so a box the text exactly fills has
     * nothing left to centre and both versions draw the same row. The column's
     * width is still the bound, because a line wider than the column is a line
     * {@link Layout#wrap} should have split and the box must not grow past what
     * the screen has room for.
     */
    private {{StringWidget}} label(String text, int y) {
        int box = Math.min({{Screen#font}}.{{Font#width}}(text), Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH);
        return new {{StringWidget}}(
                centre(), y, box, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT, {{Component}}.{{Component#literal}}(text), {{Screen#font}});
    }

    /** The entry that opens one mod's settings screen. */
    private {{Button}} settings(String owner, int y) {
        final String named = owner;
        return {{Button}}.{{Button#builder}}(
                        {{Component}}.{{Component#literal}}("Settings: " + named),
                        new {{Button$OnPress}}() {
                            @Override
                            public void {{Button$OnPress#onPress}}({{Button}} pressed) {
                                {{Minecraft}}.{{Minecraft#getInstance}}()
                                        .{{Minecraft#setScreen}}(RavelMenu.openSettings(named, RavelModsScreen.this));
                            }
                        })
                .{{Button$Builder#bounds}}(centre(), y, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT)
                .{{Button$Builder#build}}();
    }

    private int centre() {
        return ({{Screen#width}} - Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH) / 2;
    }

    /** A count and the word that agrees with it. */
    private static String plural(int count, String one, String many) {
        return count + " " + (count == 1 ? one : many);
    }

    /**
     * One row below the summary.
     *
     * <p>A row is a line of text or the settings entry for one mod, and the two
     * are one list rather than two because the window that scrolls over them
     * spans both.
     */
    private static final class Row {

        /** The text this row draws, or {@code null} if it is a settings entry. */
        private final String text;

        /** The mod this row opens the settings of, or {@code null} if it is text. */
        private final String owner;

        private Row(String text, String owner) {
            this.text = text;
            this.owner = owner;
        }

        static Row line(String text) {
            return new Row(text, null);
        }

        static Row settings(String owner) {
            return new Row(null, owner);
        }

        /** What this row reads as, which is what a settings entry is labelled. */
        String label() {
            return owner == null ? text : "Settings: " + owner;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Reads the payload into entries.
     *
     * @return whether the whole payload read
     */
    private boolean parse(String payload) {
        if (payload == null) {
            return false;
        }
        Wire wire = new Wire(payload);
        String version = wire.field();
        if (version == null || !version.equals(Wire.MODS_VERSION)) {
            return false;
        }
        String reportedSide = wire.field();
        int reportedActionable = wire.count();
        int count = wire.count();
        if (reportedSide == null || reportedActionable < 0 || count < 0) {
            return false;
        }

        List<Entry> pending = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int index = 0; index < count; index++) {
            Entry entry = Entry.read(wire);
            if (entry == null) {
                return false;
            }
            pending.add(entry);
        }

        int noticed = wire.count();
        if (noticed < 0) {
            return false;
        }
        List<String> told = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int index = 0; index < noticed; index++) {
            String notice = wire.field();
            if (notice == null) {
                return false;
            }
            told.add(notice);
        }
        if (!wire.finished()) {
            return false;
        }

        side = reportedSide;
        actionable = reportedActionable;
        entries.addAll(pending);
        notices.addAll(told);
        return true;
    }

    /** One mod, as the loader described it. */
    public static final class Entry {

        int position;
        String modId = "";
        String state = "";
        String requirement = "";
        boolean loadsHere;
        boolean actionable;
        boolean configurable;
        boolean failed;
        String kind = "";
        String surfaceKind = "";
        String surfaceName = "";
        String failedIn = "";
        String version = "";
        List<String> chain = List.of();
        String detail = "";

        /** Reads one entry, or {@code null} if the payload runs out. */
        static Entry read(Wire wire) {
            Entry entry = new Entry();
            entry.position = wire.count();
            entry.modId = wire.field();
            entry.state = wire.field();
            entry.requirement = wire.field();
            String here = wire.field();
            String act = wire.field();
            String configurable = wire.field();
            int failures = wire.count();
            if (entry.position < 0
                    || entry.modId == null
                    || entry.state == null
                    || entry.requirement == null
                    || here == null
                    || act == null
                    || configurable == null
                    || failures < 0) {
                return null;
            }
            entry.loadsHere = here.equals("true");
            entry.actionable = act.equals("true");
            entry.configurable = configurable.equals("true");
            if (failures == 0) {
                return entry;
            }

            entry.failed = true;
            entry.kind = wire.field();
            entry.surfaceKind = wire.field();
            entry.surfaceName = wire.field();
            entry.failedIn = wire.field();
            entry.version = wire.field();
            int links = wire.count();
            if (entry.kind == null
                    || entry.surfaceKind == null
                    || entry.surfaceName == null
                    || entry.failedIn == null
                    || entry.version == null
                    || links < 0) {
                return null;
            }
            List<String> chain = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int index = 0; index < links; index++) {
                String link = wire.field();
                if (link == null) {
                    return null;
                }
                chain.add(link);
            }
            entry.chain = chain;
            entry.detail = wire.field();
            return entry.detail == null ? null : entry;
        }

        /**
         * Where this mod sits in the resolved load order, numbered from one.
         *
         * <p>Every row states it, which is what lets this screen draw actionable
         * entries first without taking the load order away from a player: the
         * order is read from the numbers rather than inferred from where a row
         * happens to sit.
         */
        public int position() {
            return position;
        }

        /** The mod id this entry describes. */
        public String modId() {
            return modId;
        }

        /** Whether a player has to act on this entry. */
        public boolean isActionable() {
            return actionable;
        }

        /**
         * The lines this entry draws, composed from its structured fields.
         *
         * <p>This is the only place in Ravel where a failure becomes a sentence.
         * Everything above it — the loader, the crossing, the wire — carries
         * attribution, so that this method can decide what a player sees without
         * anything having decided for it.
         */
        public List<String> lines() {
            StringBuilder head = new StringBuilder();
            head.append(position).append(". ").append(modId);
            if (!version.isEmpty()) {
                head.append(' ').append(version);
            }
            head.append(" — ").append(state);
            if (requirement.equals("optional")) {
                head.append(" (optional)");
            }
            if (!loadsHere) {
                head.append(" (not on this side)");
            }
            if (!failed) {
                return List.of(head.toString());
            }
            head.append(actionable ? "  [needs attention]" : "  [reported]");

            StringBuilder why = new StringBuilder("    ");
            why.append(kind.isEmpty() ? "failed in " + failedIn : kind);
            if (!surfaceKind.isEmpty()) {
                why.append(" at ").append(surfaceKind).append(' ').append(surfaceName);
            }
            if (chain.size() > 1) {
                why.append(" via ").append(String.join(" -> ", chain));
            }
            if (!detail.isEmpty()) {
                why.append(": ").append(detail);
            }
            return List.of(head.toString(), why.toString());
        }
    }
}
